                        "source_sense_id": "ad2b7345-64cc-3c41-8539-3228e12bbac4",
                        "source_sense_description": "Ichthyologie: Rochen mit stark verlängerter, doppelseitig wie ein Sägeblatt gezähnter Schnauze",
                        "target_lang_word": "sawfish",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "sawfish",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "20ba3516-cb99-30d7-83b3-fa6d47289c38",
                                        "entry_id": "43aba768-6769-350e-abc3-da68042dbb6c",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Any ray (marine fish with a flat body and wing-like fins) of the family Pristidae, having a snout that resembles a saw."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "c4ac5dae-4a03-4fe9-8baa-8b71afa44937",
                                                "sense_id": "20ba3516-cb99-30d7-83b3-fa6d47289c38",
                                                "text": "2002, Maurice Burton, Robert Burton, Sawfish, entry in International Wildlife Encyclopedia, Volume 16: Rifleman - sea slug, page 2238,\nThe sawlike snout that gives the sawfish its common name may represent up to one-third of its total body length. Despite its sharklike shape, the sawfish is more closely related to rays."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "6da04860-93da-4036-b2d7-1823410feaa0",
                                                "sense_id": "20ba3516-cb99-30d7-83b3-fa6d47289c38",
                                                "text": "Because of their sharklike appearance, people often refer to the sawfishes as sharks. However, they are actually related to the skates and rays because the gills are ventral, the eyes are on top of the head, and the pectoral fins are attached to the head."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "4364a937-152a-4f3a-956c-583a302f20e2",
                                                "sense_id": "20ba3516-cb99-30d7-83b3-fa6d47289c38",
                                                "text": "Smalltooth sawfish native to rivers in south-west Florida appear to have given birth without sex – essentially cloning themselves.\nThe sawfish (Pristis pectinata) – a type of ray that grows up to 7 metres long – is a critically endangered species.[…]The sawfish is the first vertebrate shown to use parthenogenesis in the wild."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
